{"id": "trm:agama-jain", "term": "āgama (Jain canon)", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "The canonical scripture: the words of the Tīrthaṅkara as composed by the gaṇadharas and elders, fixed at Valabhī — forty-five texts for Mūrtipūjakas, thirty-two for Sthānakavāsīs and Terāpanthīs.", "sources": ["src:svetambara-agamas", "src:nandi-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:nandi-sutra:dvadasanga", "tea:avasyaka-niryukti:92"]}, {"lineage": "lin:digambara", "definition": "The Digambaras hold the original āgama (the Aṅgas and Pūrvas) lost; the Ṣaṭkhaṇḍāgama and Kaṣāyapāhuḍa preserve parts of it and are called 'āgama', and the works of later ācāryas in the four anuyogas serve as scripture.", "sources": ["src:satkhandagama", "src:srutavatara-indranandi"], "rests_on": ["tea:tiloyapannatti:4/2", "tea:srutavatara-indranandi:whole"]}], "native": "आगम", "literal": "what has come down; tradition", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "āgama", "grade": "exact"}, {"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "siddhānta", "grade": "partial", "note": "Śvetāmbara synonym for the canon"}, {"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "sūtra", "grade": "partial", "note": "common name for canonical texts"}], "related": ["trm:anga-jain", "trm:purva", "trm:sruta-jnana"], "concepts": ["cpt:svetambara-canon-45", "cpt:loss-of-scripture-digambara"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:anga-jain", "term": "aṅga (limb of the Jain canon)", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "One of the twelve 'limbs' of the canon (dvādaśāṅga, the 'basket of the gaṇin') composed by the gaṇadharas; eleven survive for the Śvetāmbaras, the twelfth (Dṛṣṭivāda) is lost.", "sources": ["src:samavayanga", "src:nandi-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:samavayanga:dvadasanga", "tea:nandi-sutra:dvadasanga"]}], "native": "अङ्ग", "literal": "limb", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "aṃga", "grade": "exact"}, {"language": "Prakrit", "form": "duvālasaṃga gaṇipiḍaga", "grade": "partial", "note": "the twelve limbs as a whole"}], "concepts": ["cpt:eleven-angas"], "notes": "Disambiguated from other senses of 'aṅga' (limb of yoga; Vīraśaiva devotee).", "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:upanga", "term": "upāṅga", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "A 'subsidiary limb': one of twelve aṅga-external canonical texts, each paired with an Aṅga (Aupapātika with the Ācārāṅga, and so on).", "sources": ["src:svetambara-agamas"]}], "native": "उपाङ्ग", "literal": "secondary limb", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "uvaṃga", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:svetambara-canon-45"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:chedasutra", "term": "chedasūtra", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "A canonical text of monastic law, fixing the atonements for transgressions; named from 'cheda' (cutting of seniority), one of the atonements. Six for Mūrtipūjakas, four for Sthānakavāsīs; traditionally studied only by mature monks.", "sources": ["src:nisitha", "src:vyavahara-sutra", "src:brhatkalpa-sutra"]}], "native": "छेदसूत्र", "literal": "text on cutting (of seniority)", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "cheyasutta", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:svetambara-canon-45"], "related": ["trm:prayascitta"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:mulasutra", "term": "mūlasūtra", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "A 'root' text studied first by new monks — the Uttarādhyayana, Daśavaikālika, Āvaśyaka and Piṇḍa- or Ogha-niryukti (Mūrtipūjaka list); the Sthānakavāsīs count the Nandī and Anuyogadvāra here instead.", "sources": ["src:uttaradhyayana-sutra", "src:dasavaikalika-sutra"]}], "native": "मूलसूत्र", "literal": "root text", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "mūlasutta", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:svetambara-canon-45"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:prakirnaka", "term": "prakīrṇaka", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:murtipujaka", "definition": "A 'miscellaneous' canonical text, ten in the standard Mūrtipūjaka list (Catuḥśaraṇa, Āturapratyākhyāna and others), many on devotion and the discipline of dying; the Nandī says there were as many prakīrṇakas as disciples of a Tīrthaṅkara. Rejected by Sthānakavāsīs and Terāpanthīs.", "sources": ["src:catuhsarana-prakirnaka", "src:nandi-sutra"]}], "native": "प्रकीर्णक", "literal": "scattered, miscellaneous", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "paiṇṇaya", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:svetambara-canon-45"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:culikasutra", "term": "cūlikāsūtra", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:murtipujaka", "definition": "An 'appendix' text of the canon: the Nandī and the Anuyogadvāra, which introduce knowledge and the method of exposition.", "sources": ["src:nandi-sutra", "src:anuyogadvara-sutra"]}], "native": "चूलिकासूत्र", "literal": "crest / appendix text", "concepts": ["cpt:svetambara-canon-45"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:purva", "term": "pūrva", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "One of the fourteen 'former' texts, said to have been taught before the Aṅgas and contained in the Dṛṣṭivāda; known in full last by the śrutakevalin Bhadrabāhu and then gradually lost.", "sources": ["src:samavayanga", "src:parisistaparvan"], "rests_on": ["tea:parisistaparvan:9", "tea:samavayanga:dvadasanga"]}, {"lineage": "lin:digambara", "definition": "Portions of the second and fifth Pūrvas survive as the Ṣaṭkhaṇḍāgama and Kaṣāyapāhuḍa.", "sources": ["src:satkhandagama", "src:kasayapahuda"], "rests_on": ["tea:kasayapahuda:1", "tea:dhavala:introduction"]}], "native": "पूर्व", "literal": "former", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "puvva", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:fourteen-purvas"], "related": ["trm:srutakevalin"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:srutakevalin", "term": "śrutakevalin", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "One who knows the whole scripture (all the Aṅgas and fourteen Pūrvas) and so is 'omniscient in scripture' though not by direct knowledge; the last was Bhadrabāhu.", "sources": ["src:parisistaparvan", "src:tiloyapannatti"], "rests_on": ["tea:tiloyapannatti:4/2"]}], "native": "श्रुतकेवली", "literal": "omniscient through scripture", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "suyakevalī", "grade": "exact"}], "related": ["trm:purva", "trm:kevalin"], "concepts": ["cpt:digambara-sruta-lineage-683"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:angapravista-angabahya", "term": "aṅgapraviṣṭa / aṅgabāhya", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "The Nandī's twofold division of scripture: what 'enters the limbs' (the twelve Aṅgas, composed by the gaṇadharas) and what is 'outside the limbs' (composed by later elders); the latter is further divided into kālika (read at set times) and utkālika (read at any time).", "sources": ["src:nandi-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:nandi-sutra:dvadasanga"]}], "native": "अङ्गप्रविष्ट / अङ्गबाह्य", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "aṃgapaviṭṭha / aṃgabāhira", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:svetambara-canon-45"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:vacana", "term": "vācanā (council)", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "A 'recitation' — an assembly of monks to collect and fix the scripture: at Pāṭaliputra after the famine, at Mathurā under Skandila (and at Valabhī under Nāgārjuna), and at Valabhī under Devarddhigaṇi, where the canon was written down.", "sources": ["src:parisistaparvan", "src:kalpa-sutra-jain"], "rests_on": ["tea:parisistaparvan:9"]}], "native": "वाचना", "literal": "recitation, reading", "concepts": ["cpt:jain-councils"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:niryukti", "term": "niryukti", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "The oldest layer of commentary on the canon: Prakrit verses (gāthā) explaining key words by the method of nikṣepa and adding narratives; ten are ascribed to Bhadrabāhu. Followed by the bhāṣya (verse), cūrṇi (Prakrit-Sanskrit prose) and ṭīkā or vṛtti (Sanskrit) layers.", "sources": ["src:avasyaka-niryukti"], "rests_on": ["tea:avasyaka-niryukti:92"]}], "native": "निर्युक्ति", "literal": "unfolding (of meaning)", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "nijjutti", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:jain-exegetical-layers"], "related": ["trm:curni"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:curni", "term": "cūrṇi", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "A prose commentary in mixed Prakrit and Sanskrit on a canonical text and its niryukti or bhāṣya (e.g. Jinadāsa's on the Niśītha and Āvaśyaka), rich in narrative.", "sources": ["src:nisitha-curni", "src:avasyaka-curni"]}], "native": "चूर्णि", "literal": "powder; fine exposition", "concepts": ["cpt:jain-exegetical-layers"], "related": ["trm:niryukti"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:ardhamagadhi", "term": "Ardhamāgadhī", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "The 'half-Māgadhī' Prakrit of the Śvetāmbara canon, held to be the language in which Mahāvīra taught; the Jina's speech in it is understood by gods, humans and animals each in their own tongue (an atiśaya).", "sources": ["src:samavayanga", "src:aupapatika"], "rests_on": ["tea:samavayanga:34"]}], "native": "अर्धमागधी", "literal": "half-Māgadhī", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "addhamāgahā", "grade": "exact"}], "related": ["trm:divyadhvani"], "concepts": ["cpt:thirty-four-atisayas"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:pahuda", "term": "pāhuḍa (prābhṛta)", "language": "Prakrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:digambara", "definition": "A 'gift' or treatise-section: in the Pūrvas, a subdivision of a vastu (the Kaṣāyapāhuḍa is such a prābhṛta); in Kundakunda's usage, a treatise (Samayapāhuḍa, the eight Pāhuḍas). Tradition credits Kundakunda with eighty-four pāhuḍas.", "sources": ["src:kasayapahuda", "src:astapahuda"], "rests_on": ["tea:kasayapahuda:1"]}], "native": "पाहुड", "literal": "gift, present", "cross_language": [{"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "prābhṛta", "grade": "exact"}], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:jita", "term": "jīta (established practice)", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "The fifth and last ground of monastic judgment: the practice established by accepted teachers, used when no higher authority (supernormal knowledge, scripture, instruction, tradition) is available; the Jītakalpa fixes atonements by it.", "sources": ["src:vyavahara-sutra", "src:jitakalpa-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:vyavahara-sutra:10"]}], "native": "जीत", "literal": "established, customary", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "jīya", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:five-vyavaharas"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:sthapana", "term": "sthāpanā (representation)", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "In the fourfold nikṣepa, a word applied to a representation of the thing — an image, painting or token — whether it resembles it or not.", "sources": ["src:anuyogadvara-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:anuyogadvara-sutra:niksepa"]}, {"lineage": "lin:murtipujaka", "definition": "The canonical ground for venerating the Jina 'placed' in an image (sthāpanā-jina), and for the sthāpanācārya (a token of the teacher used in rites).", "sources": ["src:anuyogadvara-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:anuyogadvara-sutra:niksepa"]}], "native": "स्थापना", "literal": "placing", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "ṭhavaṇā", "grade": "exact"}], "related": ["trm:niksepa"], "concepts": ["cpt:four-niksepas", "cpt:jina-image-worship"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:margana", "term": "mārgaṇā", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:digambara", "definition": "One of the fourteen 'ways of search' through which souls are investigated in the Ṣaṭkhaṇḍāgama and Gommaṭasāra (destiny, sense, body, activity, sex, passion, knowledge, restraint, perception, leśyā, capacity for liberation, right faith, rationality, nourishment).", "sources": ["src:satkhandagama", "src:gommatasara-jivakanda"], "rests_on": ["tea:satkhandagama:1.1.4"]}], "native": "मार्गणा", "literal": "search, inquiry", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "maggaṇā", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:fourteen-marganas"], "related": ["trm:gunasthana"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:digambara", "term": "digambara", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:digambara", "definition": "'Sky-clad': the naked monk, and the tradition that holds complete nudity necessary for the fully renounced and for liberation.", "sources": ["src:astapahuda", "src:mulacara"], "rests_on": ["tea:astapahuda:sutta.23", "tea:mulacara:1.2-3"]}, {"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "Called Boṭika (Boḍiya) in Śvetāmbara sources, which trace it to Śivabhūti's schism.", "sources": ["src:visesavasyaka-bhasya"], "rests_on": ["tea:visesavasyaka-bhasya:botika"]}], "native": "दिगम्बर", "literal": "sky-clad", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "diyaṃbara", "grade": "exact"}, {"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "nirgrantha", "grade": "partial", "note": "self-designation"}, {"language": "Tamil", "form": "tikampara", "grade": "exact", "note": "low confidence on form"}], "related": ["trm:svetambara", "trm:acela", "trm:nirgrantha"], "concepts": ["cpt:monastic-nudity"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:svetambara", "term": "śvetāmbara", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "'White-clad': the tradition whose monks and nuns wear white garments, keeping clothes and bowls as instruments of restraint, not as possessions.", "sources": ["src:dasavaikalika-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:dasavaikalika-sutra:6.20-21"]}, {"lineage": "lin:digambara", "definition": "Called Śvetapaṭa in Digambara sources, which trace it to a famine-time relaxation of practice.", "sources": ["src:darsanasara-devasena", "src:bhadrabahucarita"], "rests_on": ["tea:darsanasara-devasena:sec.svetambara", "tea:bhadrabahucarita:whole"]}], "native": "श्वेताम्बर", "literal": "white-clad", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "seyaṃbara", "grade": "exact"}, {"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "śvetapaṭa", "grade": "exact", "note": "Digambara usage"}], "related": ["trm:digambara"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:yapaniya", "term": "Yāpanīya", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:yapaniya", "definition": "Name of the extinct order that combined naked monasticism with acceptance of women's liberation and the kevalin's eating; the name has been explained from 'yāpanīya' ('that which sustains / keeps going'), a word in the formula of monastic greeting (low confidence).", "sources": ["src:strinirvana-prakarana", "src:darsanasara-devasena"], "rests_on": ["tea:darsanasara-devasena:sec.yapaniya", "tea:strinirvana-prakarana:1-2"]}], "native": "यापनीय", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "jāvaṇijja", "grade": "exact"}], "related": ["trm:strinirvana", "trm:kevalibhukti"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:murtipujaka", "term": "mūrtipūjaka", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:murtipujaka", "definition": "'Image-worshipper': the Śvetāmbaras who worship consecrated images of the Jinas in temples (also Derāvāsī, 'temple-dweller/goer', and Mandiramārgī).", "sources": ["src:rajaprasniya"], "rests_on": ["tea:rajaprasniya:suryabha"]}], "native": "मूर्तिपूजक", "literal": "image-worshipper", "cross_language": [{"language": "Gujarati", "form": "derāvāsī", "grade": "partial"}, {"language": "Hindi", "form": "mandiramārgī", "grade": "partial"}], "concepts": ["cpt:jina-image-worship"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:sthanakavasi", "term": "sthānakavāsī", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:sthanakavasi", "definition": "'Dweller in halls': the aniconic Śvetāmbaras, whose mendicants stay in plain halls (sthānaka) rather than near temples, and who reject image worship.", "sources": ["src:svetambara-agamas"], "rests_on": ["tea:acaranga-sutra:1.1.2-7"]}], "native": "स्थानकवासी", "literal": "dweller in halls", "cross_language": [{"language": "Hindi", "form": "sthānakvāsī", "grade": "exact"}, {"language": "Hindi", "form": "ḍhūṇḍhiyā", "grade": "partial", "note": "older, pejorative"}], "related": ["trm:mukhavastrika"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:terapantha", "term": "Terāpantha", "language": "Rajasthani", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:terapanthi", "definition": "The Śvetāmbara order founded by Ācārya Bhikṣu (1760); the name is explained as 'the path of thirteen' (after the first thirteen monks or laymen) or as 'your path, O Lord' (terā = 'yours').", "sources": ["src:bhiksu-granth-ratnakar"], "rests_on": ["tea:bhiksu-granth-ratnakar:anukampa"]}, {"lineage": "lin:digambara", "definition": "Also the name of an unconnected 17th-c. Digambara reform (against the bhaṭṭārakas and fresh offerings), contrasted with the Bīsapantha.", "sources": ["src:moksamarga-prakasaka"], "rests_on": ["tea:moksamarga-prakasaka:7"]}], "native": "तेरापंथ", "cross_language": [{"language": "Hindi", "form": "terāpanth", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:terapanthi-maryada"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:gaccha", "term": "gaccha", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:murtipujaka", "definition": "A monastic lineage or order among the Śvetāmbara Mūrtipūjakas (Tapā, Kharatara, Añcala and others), with its own succession of ācāryas, ritual details and calendar; earlier sources use gaṇa, kula and śākhā.", "sources": ["src:kalpa-sutra-jain"], "rests_on": ["tea:kalpa-sutra-jain:sthaviravali"]}], "native": "गच्छ", "literal": "going; group", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "gaccha", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:gaccha-system"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:caturvidha-sangha", "term": "caturvidha saṅgha", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "The fourfold community founded by each Tīrthaṅkara: monks (sādhu), nuns (sādhvī), laymen (śrāvaka) and laywomen (śrāvikā); itself called the 'ford' (tīrtha).", "sources": ["src:kalpa-sutra-jain"], "rests_on": ["tea:kalpa-sutra-jain:jinacaritra.mahavira/3"]}], "native": "चतुर्विध संघ", "literal": "fourfold community", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "cauvviha saṃgha", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:fourfold-sangha"], "related": ["trm:tirthankara"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:bhattaraka", "term": "bhaṭṭāraka", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:digambara", "definition": "A clothed Digambara cleric heading a seat (maṭha), who administers temples, institutions and learning and conducts rites; authority of the Bīsapantha, rejected by the Digambara Terāpantha; seats at Śravaṇabeḷagoḷa, Mūḍabidri, Kolhapur and elsewhere.", "sources": ["src:moksamarga-prakasaka"]}], "native": "भट्टारक", "literal": "venerable lord", "concepts": ["cpt:bhattaraka-institution"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:caityavasa", "term": "caityavāsa", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:murtipujaka", "definition": "The practice of monks residing permanently in temples and managing their property, attacked by Haribhadra and by the Kharatara reformers, who insisted on vasativāsa, living in lodgings offered by laypeople.", "sources": ["src:avasyaka-vrtti-haribhadra"]}], "native": "चैत्यवास", "literal": "temple-dwelling", "related": ["trm:gaccha"], "concepts": ["cpt:caityavasa"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:yati-jain", "term": "yati (Śvetāmbara)", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "A clothed resident Śvetāmbara religious of the late medieval period, less strict than a full mendicant, often keeping property and practising astrology or medicine; the reformers who founded the Sthānakavāsīs and revived Mūrtipūjaka mendicancy reacted against them."}], "native": "यति", "literal": "restrained one", "related": ["trm:sadhu"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:nihnava", "term": "nihnava", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "A 'concealer' (schismatic) who, while accepting the Jina's teaching, denied one point of it and would not be corrected — seven in the Sthānāṅga (from Jamāli to Goṣṭhāmāhila), eight with the Boṭikas in the Āvaśyaka commentaries.", "sources": ["src:sthananga-sutra", "src:visesavasyaka-bhasya"], "rests_on": ["tea:sthananga-sutra:7", "tea:visesavasyaka-bhasya:botika"]}], "native": "निह्नव", "literal": "concealer, denier", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "ninhava", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:nihnavas"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:botika", "term": "Boṭika", "language": "Prakrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "Śvetāmbara name for the Digambaras, as followers of Śivabhūti's schism of 609 years after the nirvāṇa.", "sources": ["src:visesavasyaka-bhasya"], "rests_on": ["tea:visesavasyaka-bhasya:botika"]}], "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "boḍiya", "grade": "exact"}], "related": ["trm:digambara", "trm:nihnava"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:maryada", "term": "maryādā", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:terapanthi", "definition": "The written code of the Terāpanth order laid down by Ācārya Bhikṣu (a single ācārya, no personal disciples, collective discipline), celebrated in the annual Maryādā Mahotsava (from Jayācārya's time).", "sources": ["src:bhiksu-granth-ratnakar"]}], "native": "मर्यादा", "literal": "limit, boundary, rule", "concepts": ["cpt:terapanthi-maryada"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:tirthankara", "term": "tīrthaṅkara", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "A 'ford-maker': an omniscient Jina who, after attaining kevala, teaches the dharma and founds the fourfold community (the 'ford' across saṃsāra); twenty-four appear in each half of the time-cycle, by the ripening of the tīrthaṅkara-nāma-karma bound in an earlier life.", "sources": ["src:kalpa-sutra-jain", "src:avasyaka-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:avasyaka-sutra:2", "tea:kalpa-sutra-jain:jinacaritra.mahavira/2"]}, {"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "The Śvetāmbaras include a woman, Mallī, among the Tīrthaṅkaras of this era.", "sources": ["src:jnatadharmakatha"], "rests_on": ["tea:jnatadharmakatha:1.8"]}, {"lineage": "lin:digambara", "definition": "All Tīrthaṅkaras are male; the Tīrthaṅkara does not eat after omniscience.", "sources": ["src:tiloyapannatti", "src:niyamasara"], "rests_on": ["tea:niyamasara:6"]}], "native": "तीर्थङ्कर", "literal": "ford-maker", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "titthayara", "grade": "exact"}, {"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "tīrthakara", "grade": "exact", "note": "variant form used e.g. in Tattvārthasūtra 6.24"}, {"language": "Tamil", "form": "tīrttaṅkarar", "grade": "exact"}], "equivalents": [{"target": "trm:tirthakara", "grade": "exact", "note": "the same word in its variant form; the Pāśupata use of 'tīrthakara' for their own teachers is a different referent", "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:6.24"]}], "related": ["trm:jina", "trm:arhat", "trm:kevalin", "trm:caturvidha-sangha", "trm:tirthankara-nama-karma"], "concepts": ["cpt:twenty-four-tirthankaras", "cpt:five-kalyanakas"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:tirthakara", "term": "tīrthakara", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "Variant of tīrthaṅkara, the ford-making Jina (used in the Digambara Tattvārthasūtra 6.24).", "sources": ["src:tattvartha-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:tattvartha-sutra:6.24"]}], "native": "तीर्थकर", "related": ["trm:tirthankara"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:ganadhara", "term": "gaṇadhara", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "A 'holder of the group': one of the chief disciples of a Tīrthaṅkara, who hears his teaching and composes it as scripture and leads a section of the community; Mahāvīra had eleven, headed by Indrabhūti Gautama.", "sources": ["src:avasyaka-niryukti", "src:visesavasyaka-bhasya"], "rests_on": ["tea:avasyaka-niryukti:92", "tea:visesavasyaka-bhasya:ganadharavada/2"]}], "native": "गणधर", "literal": "bearer of the group", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "gaṇahara", "grade": "exact"}], "concepts": ["cpt:ganadharavada", "cpt:jain-scripture-origin"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:sadhu", "term": "sādhu (Jain mendicant)", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "A fully initiated Jain monk who has taken the five great vows for life; the fifth rank saluted in the Namaskāra ('all the sādhus in the world').", "sources": ["src:dasavaikalika-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:dasavaikalika-sutra:10", "tea:bhagavati-sutra:mangala"]}], "native": "साधु", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "sāhu", "grade": "exact"}, {"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "muni", "grade": "exact"}, {"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "śramaṇa / nirgrantha", "grade": "partial"}, {"language": "Gujarati", "form": "mahārāj-sāheb", "grade": "partial", "note": "respectful address"}], "related": ["trm:sadhvi", "trm:muni", "trm:paramesthin"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:sadhvi", "term": "sādhvī", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:svetambara", "definition": "A fully initiated Jain nun with the five great vows; in Śvetāmbara orders nuns outnumber monks, are led by a pravartinī and follow rules such as never going unclothed.", "sources": ["src:brhatkalpa-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:brhatkalpa-sutra:5"]}, {"lineage": "lin:digambara", "definition": "The Digambara nun is called āryikā; she keeps one white sari and is not regarded as fully without possessions.", "sources": ["src:mulacara"], "rests_on": ["tea:mulacara:4"]}], "native": "साध्वी", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "sāhuṇī", "grade": "exact"}, {"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "āryikā", "grade": "partial", "note": "Digambara term"}, {"language": "Hindi", "form": "mahāsatī", "grade": "partial", "note": "Sthānakavāsī usage"}], "related": ["trm:aryika", "trm:sadhu"], "concepts": ["cpt:strimukti"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:aryika", "term": "āryikā", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:digambara", "definition": "A Digambara nun: she wears a single white garment, lives with other nuns, eats seated (not in the hand like the monk), and in Digambara teaching cannot in this body pass beyond the fifth stage in the full sense because she keeps a garment.", "sources": ["src:mulacara"], "rests_on": ["tea:mulacara:4"]}], "native": "आर्यिका", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "ajjiyā", "grade": "exact"}], "related": ["trm:sadhvi", "trm:ksullaka"], "concepts": ["cpt:strimukti"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:ksullaka", "term": "kṣullaka / ailaka", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:digambara", "definition": "The advanced Digambara lay renunciants of the eleventh pratimā: the kṣullaka keeps two cloths and a bowl, the ailaka a single loincloth and eats from the hand; the next step is muni-initiation into nudity. Women may become kṣullikās.", "sources": ["src:mulacara"]}], "native": "क्षुल्लक / ऐलक", "literal": "junior (one)", "paths": [], "concepts": ["cpt:eleven-pratimas"], "related": ["trm:aryika", "trm:muni"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:sravika", "term": "śrāvikā", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "A Jain laywoman, fourth member of the fourfold community; the Kalpa Sūtra counts 318,000 in Mahāvīra's time, headed by Sulasā and Revatī.", "sources": ["src:kalpa-sutra-jain"], "rests_on": ["tea:kalpa-sutra-jain:jinacaritra.mahavira/3"]}], "native": "श्राविका", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "sāviyā", "grade": "exact"}], "related": ["trm:sravaka"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:salakapurusa", "term": "śalākāpuruṣa", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "An 'illustrious person' of the universal history: in each half-cycle sixty-three — 24 Tīrthaṅkaras, 12 cakravartins, 9 baladevas, 9 vāsudevas and 9 prativāsudevas.", "sources": ["src:trisastisalakapurusacaritra", "src:adipurana-jinasena"], "rests_on": ["tea:trisastisalakapurusacaritra:1"]}], "native": "शलाकापुरुष", "literal": "person marked (with a rod / tally)", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "salāgapurisa", "grade": "exact"}, {"language": "Prakrit", "form": "mahāpurisa", "grade": "partial"}], "concepts": ["cpt:sixty-three-salakapurusas"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:vasudeva-jain", "term": "vāsudeva (Jain)", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "One of the nine half-world-conquerors of each half-cycle, always paired with an elder brother baladeva and opposed by a prativāsudeva whom he kills; Lakṣmaṇa and Kṛṣṇa are the eighth and ninth. Because of the violence of their lives vāsudevas go to hell, though Kṛṣṇa will be a future Tīrthaṅkara.", "sources": ["src:trisastisalakapurusacaritra", "src:harivamsapurana-jinasena"], "rests_on": ["tea:paumacariya:2"]}], "native": "वासुदेव", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "vāsudeva", "grade": "exact"}, {"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "nārāyaṇa", "grade": "exact", "note": "Digambara usage"}, {"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "ardhacakrin", "grade": "partial"}], "related": ["trm:salakapurusa"], "concepts": ["cpt:sixty-three-salakapurusas"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:kulakara", "term": "kulakara", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "One of the patriarchs who, at the end of the age of wish-fulfilling trees, guided people and instituted the first laws; the last was Nābhi, father of Ṛṣabha (lists of seven or fourteen).", "sources": ["src:jambudvipaprajnapti", "src:adipurana-jinasena"], "rests_on": ["tea:jambudvipaprajnapti:2"]}], "native": "कुलकर", "literal": "maker of clans", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "kulagara", "grade": "exact"}, {"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "manu", "grade": "partial", "note": "Digambara usage"}], "concepts": ["cpt:jain-time-cycle"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:viharamana", "term": "viharamāṇa (living) Tīrthaṅkara", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "A Tīrthaṅkara now teaching in the Mahāvideha regions, where the fourth age always prevails; twenty are counted, the first being Sīmandhara.", "sources": ["src:darsanasara-devasena"], "rests_on": ["tea:darsanasara-devasena:sec.kundakunda"]}], "native": "विहरमाण", "literal": "wandering (still living)", "concepts": ["cpt:viharamana-tirthankaras"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:nirgrantha", "term": "nirgrantha", "language": "Sanskrit", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:jainism", "definition": "'Knotless' — free of the knots of possessions and passions; the old name of Jain mendicants (Pali 'nigaṇṭha') and of Mahāvīra as Nigaṇṭha Nātaputta; Digambaras take it as requiring nudity.", "sources": ["src:acaranga-sutra"], "rests_on": ["tea:acaranga-sutra:1.2.6"]}], "native": "निर्ग्रन्थ", "literal": "without knots", "cross_language": [{"language": "Prakrit", "form": "niggaṃtha", "grade": "exact"}, {"language": "Pali", "form": "nigaṇṭha", "grade": "exact", "note": "as used in the Pali canon of Jain monks"}], "related": ["trm:digambara", "trm:aparigraha"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:amanar", "term": "amaṇar / camaṇar", "language": "Tamil", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:tamil-jain", "definition": "Tamil name for Jain monks (from śramaṇa), common in Tamil literature and in the hymns of the Nāyaṉmārs who opposed them.", "sources": ["src:naladiyar"]}], "cross_language": [{"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "śramaṇa", "grade": "exact"}], "related": ["trm:sramana"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:arukan", "term": "Arukaṉ", "language": "Tamil", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:tamil-jain", "definition": "Tamil form of Arhat, the Jina, invoked in the opening verses of Tamil Jain works.", "sources": ["src:nannul", "src:civakacintamani"]}], "cross_language": [{"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "arhat", "grade": "exact"}], "related": ["trm:arhat"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:aram", "term": "aṟam", "language": "Tamil", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:tamil-jain", "definition": "Virtue, dharma: in the Jain didactic works (Nālaṭiyār) the right conduct to be done now, before wealth, youth and the body pass away.", "sources": ["src:naladiyar"], "rests_on": ["tea:naladiyar:adh.3"]}], "cross_language": [{"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "dharma", "grade": "partial"}], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
{"id": "trm:turavu", "term": "tuṟavu", "language": "Tamil", "definitions": [{"lineage": "lin:tamil-jain", "definition": "Renunciation: in the Nālaṭiyār the wise one's giving up of attachment on seeing the body's impurity and the world's transience.", "sources": ["src:naladiyar"], "rests_on": ["tea:naladiyar:adh.6"]}], "cross_language": [{"language": "Sanskrit", "form": "tyāga / pravrajyā", "grade": "partial"}], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}}
